ostituted itself. You have also killed your brothers, your father’s family, who were better than you.","verse":13}],"id":"Ahab","label":"Ahab","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Ahab::Ahab::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Ahab","term":"Ahab"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Ahab","sourceTitle":"Ahab"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"David","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"David::David::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"David"},"text":"And Jehoshaphat rested with his fathers and was buried with them in the City of David. And his son Jehoram reigned in his place.","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"David","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"David::David::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"David"},"text":"Yet the LORD was unwilling to destroy the house of David, because of the covenant He had made with David, and since He had promised to maintain a lamp for David and his descendants forever.","verse":7},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"David","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"David::David::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"David"},"text":"Then a letter came to Jehoram from Elijah the prophet, which stated: “This is what the LORD, the God of your father David, says: ‘You have not walked in the ways of your father Jehoshaphat or of Asa king of Judah,","verse":12},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"David","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"David::David::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"David"},"text":"Jehoram was thirty-two years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem eight years. He died, to no one’s regret, and was buried in the City of David, but not in the tombs of the kings.","verse":20}],"id":"David","label":"David","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"David::David::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"David","term":"David"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"David","sourceTitle":"David"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Elijah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":13,"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.12-15","rawReference":"2Chr.21.12-15"}],"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah"},"text":"Then a letter came to Jehoram from Elijah the prophet, which stated: “This is what the LORD, the God of your father David, says: ‘You have not walked in the ways of your father Jehoshaphat or of Asa king of Judah,","verse":12}],"id":"Elijah","label":"Elijah","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Elijah::Elijah::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Elijah","term":"Elijah"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Elijah","sourceTitle":"Elijah"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jerusalem","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem"},"text":"Jehoram was thirty-two years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem eight years.","verse":5},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jerusalem","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem"},"text":"Jehoram had also built high places on the hills of Judah; he had caused the people of Jerusalem to prostitute themselves and had led Judah astray.","verse":11},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jerusalem","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem"},"text":"but you have walked in the ways of the kings of Israel and have caused Judah and the people of Jerusalem to prostitute themselves, just as the house of Ahab prostituted itself. You have also killed your brothers, your father’s family, who were better than you.","verse":13},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Jerusalem","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem"},"text":"Jehoram was thirty-two years old when he became king, and he reigned in Jerusalem eight years. He died, to no one’s regret, and was buried in the City of David, but not in the tombs of the kings.","verse":20}],"id":"Jerusalem","label":"Jerusalem","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Jerusalem::Jerusalem::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Jerusalem","term":"Jerusalem"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Jerusalem","sourceTitle":"Jerusalem"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"And Jehoram walked in the ways of the kings of Israel, just as the house of Ahab had done. For he married a daughter of Ahab and did evil in the sight of the LORD.","verse":6},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Yet the LORD was unwilling to destroy the house of David, because of the covenant He had made with David, and since He had promised to maintain a lamp for David and his descendants forever.","verse":7},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"So to this day Edom has been in rebellion against the hand of Judah. Likewise, Libnah rebelled against his hand at the same time, because Jehoram had forsaken the LORD, the God of his fathers.","verse":10},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Then a letter came to Jehoram from Elijah the prophet, which stated: “This is what the LORD, the God of your father David, says: ‘You have not walked in the ways of your father Jehoshaphat or of Asa king of Judah,","verse":12},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"So behold, the LORD is about to strike your people, your sons, your wives, and all your possessions with a serious blow.","verse":14},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Then the LORD stirred against Jehoram the spirit of the Philistines and Arabs who lived near the Cushites.","verse":16},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"After all this, the LORD afflicted Jehoram with an incurable disease of the bowels.","verse":18}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_qualified_headword","phrase":"Zechariah","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":18,"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.2","rawReference":"2Chr.21.2"}],"ruleId":"ZechariahPerson::Zechariah::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"ZechariahPerson"},"text":"Jehoram’s brothers, the sons of Jehoshaphat, were Azariah, Jehiel, Zechariah, Azariah, Michael, and Shephatiah; these were all sons of Jehoshaphat king of Israel.","verse":2}],"id":"ZechariahPerson","label":"Zechariah","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"ZechariahPerson::Zechariah::source_verse_citation","scope":"source_verse_citation","senseReview":"Original title explicitly labels the subject Person. This is a reviewed person headword, never a stripped book title; the original person article must cite the exact occurrence verse. Other people sharing the name are retained within the original multi-sense person article.","sourceHeadwordId":"ZechariahPerson","term":"Zechariah"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_qualified_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_qualified_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"ZechariahPerson","sourceTitle":"Zechariah (Person)"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":["Jehoshaphat"],"sourceClass":"profile-title","t":"h"},{"c":["Jehoshaphat succeeded his father Asa to become the fourth king of Judah (872–848 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"). Like Hezekiah and Josiah after him, Jehoshaphat trusted the Lord throughout his life and worked to remove most forms of pagan worship from Israel (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.17.6","r":"2Chr.17.6","rawR":"2Chr.17.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.17.6","value":"2Chr.17.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 17:6"},")."],"sourceClass":"profile-body-fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Jehoshaphat continued his father Asa’s religious reforms but reversed his foreign policy. Jehoshaphat discontinued Judah’s war with Israel over their boundaries (see ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.22.2","r":"1Kgs.22.2","rawR":"1Kgs.22.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.22.2","value":"1Kgs.22.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 22:2"},") and made an alliance with Ahab. To confirm this alliance, he arranged for his son Jehoram to marry Ahab’s daughter Athaliah (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.18.1-2","r":"2Chr.18.1-2","rawR":"2Chr.18.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.18.1-2","value":"2Chr.18.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 18:1-2"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.8.18","r":"2Kgs.8.18","rawR":"2Kgs.8.18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.8.18","value":"2Kgs.8.18"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 8:18"},") and supported the north in its wars against the Arameans and Moabites (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.18.3-34","r":"2Chr.18.3-34","rawR":"2Chr.18.3-34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.18.3-34","value":"2Chr.18.3-34"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 18:3-34"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.22.1-53","r":"1Kgs.22.1-53","rawR":"1Kgs.22.1-53","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.22.1-53","value":"1Kgs.22.1-53"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.3.4-27","r":"2Kgs.3.4-27","rawR":"2Kgs.3.4-27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.3.4-27","value":"2Kgs.3.4-27"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 3:4-27"},"). The prophet Jehu rebuked Jehoshaphat for his unwise alliance with Israel (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.19.1-3","r":"2Chr.19.1-3","rawR":"2Chr.19.1-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.19.1-3","value":"2Chr.19.1-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 19:1-3"},"), which resulted in his son Jehoram falling under the influence of Ahab and Jezebel. Jehoram and his son Ahaziah turned Judah toward idol worship."],"sourceClass":"profile-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Yet during his reign, Jehoshaphat kept the worship of the Lord pure. He closed the temples of prostitution (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.22.46","r":"1Kgs.22.46","rawR":"1Kgs.22.46","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.22.46","value":"1Kgs.22.46"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 22:46"},") and sent teachers of God’s law throughout the land (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.17.7-9","r":"2Chr.17.7-9","rawR":"2Chr.17.7-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.17.7-9","value":"2Chr.17.7-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 17:7-9"},"). He also established a system of judges and admonished them to function as the Lord’s representatives so that justice might prevail (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.19.4-7","r":"2Chr.19.4-7","rawR":"2Chr.19.4-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.19.4-7","value":"2Chr.19.4-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:4-7"},"). He appointed Levites, priests, and family heads to handle cases pertaining to religious practice and to negotiate disputes among citizens (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.19.8-10","r":"2Chr.19.8-10","rawR":"2Chr.19.8-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.19.8-10","value":"2Chr.19.8-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:8-10"},")."],"sourceClass":"profile-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Before going to battle, Jehoshaphat would insist on hearing from a true prophet of the Lord. And in one of the most stirring descriptions of war in the Old Testament, Jehoshaphat preached to the army before entering a battle. As a result, God caused the armies from Ammon, Moab, and their allies to turn against each other (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.20.1-37","r":"2Chr.20.1-37","rawR":"2Chr.20.1-37","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.20.1-37","value":"2Chr.20.1-37"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 20"},")."],"sourceClass":"profile-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Jehoshaphat died when he was about sixty years old and was buried in Jerusalem. His son Jehoram became Judah’s next king (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.1","r":"2Chr.21.1","rawR":"2Chr.21.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.1","value":"2Chr.21.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:1"},")."],"sourceClass":"profile-body","t":"p"},{"c":["Passages for Further Study"],"sourceClass":"profile-refs-title","t":"h"},{"c":[{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.15.24","r":"1Kgs.15.24","rawR":"1Kgs.15.24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.15.24","value":"1Kgs.15.24"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 15:24"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.22.1-50","r":"1Kgs.22.1-50","rawR":"1Kgs.22.1-50","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.22.1-50","value":"1Kgs.22.1-50"}],"t":"ref","v":"22:1-50"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.3.1-27","r":"2Kgs.3.1-27","rawR":"2Kgs.3.1-27","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.3.1-27","value":"2Kgs.3.1-27"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 3:1-27"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.17.1-21","r":"2Chr.17.1-21","rawR":"2Chr.17.1-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.17.1-21","value":"2Chr.17.1-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 17:1–21:1"}],"sourceClass":"profile-refs","t":"p"}],"id":"Profile:Jehoshaphat","refs":[{"book":"2CH","endChapter":21,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":17,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Jehoshaphat","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"Profile"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2CH","sourceChapterCoverage":[17,18,19,20,21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"4f346bc89e30a1c599aafc8ad25c1d1284fb1eed94055941ce5f3dabccd4307c","sourceItemName":"Jehoshaphat","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/Profiles.xml","sourceOrder":62,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2CH","canonicalOSIS":"2Chr","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Chr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Chr.17.1-21.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":"Jehoshaphat","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Jehoshaphat","type":"Profile"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.2-7","r":"2Chr.21.2-7","rawR":"2Chr.21.2-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.2-7","value":"2Chr.21.2-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:2-7"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Jehoram"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," whose wife was Athaliah, the daughter of Ahab and Jezebel (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.18.1","r":"2Chr.18.1","rawR":"2Chr.18.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.18.1","value":"2Chr.18.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"18:1"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.6","r":"2Chr.21.6","rawR":"2Chr.21.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.6","value":"2Chr.21.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.22.2","r":"2Chr.22.2","rawR":"2Chr.22.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.22.2","value":"2Chr.22.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"22:2"},"), was the first king of David’s line to receive a totally negative evaluation. His murders (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.4","r":"2Chr.21.4","rawR":"2Chr.21.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.4","value":"2Chr.21.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:4"},") seriously threatened the continuation of the dynasty, which was only preserved because of the Lord’s own loyal faithfulness to David (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.7","r":"2Chr.21.7","rawR":"2Chr.21.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.7","value":"2Chr.21.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:7"},"). On three other occasions, violence was perpetrated against the royal family that all but ended the dynasty (see ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.17","r":"2Chr.21.17","rawR":"2Chr.21.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.17","value":"2Chr.21.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:17"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.22.8-9","r":"2Chr.22.8-9","rawR":"2Chr.22.8-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.22.8-9","value":"2Chr.22.8-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"22:8-9"},", ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.22.10-11","r":"2Chr.22.10-11","rawR":"2Chr.22.10-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.22.10-11","value":"2Chr.22.10-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"10-11"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIChr.21.2-7","refs":[{"book":"2CH","endChapter":21,"endVerse":7,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIChr.21.2-7","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2CH","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"8c8160f9d994ba90f12ee48c86de1272e7e371624e4f13a868d8672514842563","sourceItemName":"IIChr.21.2-7","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5041,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2CH","canonicalOSIS":"2Chr","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Chr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Chr.21.2-7","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIChr.21.2-7","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.5","r":"2Chr.21.5","rawR":"2Chr.21.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.5","value":"2Chr.21.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," After a three-year co-regency with his father, ",{"c":["Jehoram"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," reigned from 848 to 841 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIChr.21.5","refs":[{"book":"2CH","endChapter":21,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIChr.21.5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2CH","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"eb1dfe8c703a411d3d8d35506cea98cc74092417f2824025d0fc64b24598080c","sourceItemName":"IIChr.21.5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5042,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2CH","canonicalOSIS":"2Chr","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Chr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Chr.21.5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIChr.21.5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.8-11","r":"2Chr.21.8-11","rawR":"2Chr.21.8-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.8-11","value":"2Chr.21.8-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:8-11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Jehoram’s"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," disobedience caused him to lose the gains that Asa and Jehoshaphat had won. • ",{"c":["the Edomites revolted:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," During Solomon’s reign, control of the territory of Edom gave Israel access to the rich trade from Arabia. The Edomites had also rebelled before Solomon’s death (",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.11.14-22","r":"1Kgs.11.14-22","rawR":"1Kgs.11.14-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.11.14-22","value":"1Kgs.11.14-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 11:14-22"},"), and were probably not under Rehoboam’s control. They apparently came under Judah’s control again following their defeat by Jehoshaphat (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.20.1-30","r":"2Chr.20.1-30","rawR":"2Chr.20.1-30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.20.1-30","value":"2Chr.20.1-30"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 20:1-30"},"; cp. ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.22.47","r":"1Kgs.22.47","rawR":"1Kgs.22.47","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.22.47","value":"1Kgs.22.47"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 22:47"},"). • The ",{"c":["town of Libnah"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was at the western end of the valley of Elah, on the border of the territory of the Philistines."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIChr.21.8-11","refs":[{"book":"2CH","endChapter":21,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":8}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIChr.21.8-11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2CH","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"fded174ef6bc1c84390898ac559a23412712a1a7ef4eec816c35b9956497910d","sourceItemName":"IIChr.21.8-11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5043,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2CH","canonicalOSIS":"2Chr","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Chr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Chr.21.8-11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIChr.21.8-11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.12-15","r":"2Chr.21.12-15","rawR":"2Chr.21.12-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.12-15","value":"2Chr.21.12-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:12-15"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The letter from ",{"c":["Elijah the prophet"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," to ",{"c":["Jehoram"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," announced judgment for his disobedience."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIChr.21.12-15","refs":[{"book":"2CH","endChapter":21,"endVerse":15,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIChr.21.12-15","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2CH","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"d589fbb2d8649d0d3675e3880f3c6aa7bbcfba376e04b64f86d045591a2a4304","sourceItemName":"IIChr.21.12-15","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5044,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2CH","canonicalOSIS":"2Chr","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Chr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Chr.21.12-15","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIChr.21.12-15","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.16-17","r":"2Chr.21.16-17","rawR":"2Chr.21.16-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.16-17","value":"2Chr.21.16-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:16-17"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Jehoram’s inability to resist the initial revolts of Edom and Libnah (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.8-10","r":"2Chr.21.8-10","rawR":"2Chr.21.8-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.8-10","value":"2Chr.21.8-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:8-10"},") encouraged other attacks on Judah, this time by ",{"c":["the Philistines and the Arabs"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},". Once again, the royal line was nearly destroyed."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIChr.21.16-17","refs":[{"book":"2CH","endChapter":21,"endVerse":17,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":16}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIChr.21.16-17","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2CH","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5b3364de7b69884045786f2abd4181f372895fe0cc8170924772c05aed25e138","sourceItemName":"IIChr.21.16-17","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5045,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2CH","canonicalOSIS":"2Chr","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Chr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Chr.21.16-17","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIChr.21.16-17","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=2Chr.21.18-20","r":"2Chr.21.18-20","rawR":"2Chr.21.18-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.21.18-20","value":"2Chr.21.18-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:18-20"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Jehoram"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," died after a long and painful bowel disease. He was not given the dignity of an honorary funeral rite (see ",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.16.14","r":"2Chr.16.14","rawR":"2Chr.16.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.16.14","value":"2Chr.16.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"16:14"},") and was buried in disgrace away from the ",{"c":["royal cemetery"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:IIChr.21.18-20","refs":[{"book":"2CH","endChapter":21,"endVerse":20,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":18}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"IIChr.21.18-20","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"2CH","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"80d4c717fc41491332c474cf268d825fa21528e1b8ddeff296c2da7dd8386382","sourceItemName":"IIChr.21.18-20","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":5046,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"2CH","canonicalOSIS":"2Chr","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"2Chr"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"2Chr.21.18-20","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"IIChr.21.18-20","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}